00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:14,480
So, to understand the solution Flyxion is proposing, we have to deeply understand the trap we are all currently caught in.

41
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:18,580
The paper calls this the identical endpoint problem.

42
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:20,140
The identical endpoint problem. Yeah.

43
00:03:20,140 --> 00:03:27,380
It is the core vulnerability of almost all modern technology. And it really stems from our obsession with present state auditing.

44
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:29,440
Right. Checking things as they are right now.

45
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:36,100
Exactly. We evaluate software, AI models, and even physical machines by checking their current status.

46
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:42,100
We look at the model's weights. We measure its current outputs. We check its behavior against a static test suite.

47
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:47,100
And the paper argues this entire framing is not just flawed, but actively deceptive.

48
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:55,920
Actively deceptive. Yeah. Because two complex systems can occupy an identical present state, but have arrived there through wildly different histories.

