00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:09,600
And this is where Flyxion introduces the core mathematical reformulation of the paper, which they call systems as histories.

87
00:07:09,780 --> 00:07:10,980
Systems as histories, yes.

88
00:07:11,140 --> 00:07:14,400
Flyxion argues we just have to stop defining a system by its current parameters.

89
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:20,080
Instead, they introduce a new notation, H-sub-sigma, standing for system history.

90
00:07:20,460 --> 00:07:29,900
Right. So, Flyxion defines a system at any given time, not as a static object, but as a constantly evolving tuple, basically a mathematical list of interconnected elements.

91
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:30,240
Right.

92
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:36,040
And this tuple includes theta-sub-t, which is the current operative parameters or weights of the model.

93
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:44,060
But crucially, it expands outward to include D-sub-t, the data dependencies the system currently relies on.

94
00:07:44,660 --> 00:07:46,840
It also integrates L-sub-t, its lineage.

95
00:07:47,040 --> 00:07:48,620
Let's unpack lineage real quick.

96
00:07:48,860 --> 00:07:56,500
Lineage being the cryptographic or documented record of how that specific data and those specific parameters were actually produced, right?

97
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:57,000
Yeah, exactly.

98
00:07:57,020 --> 00:07:59,200
It's basically the chain of custody for the code.

99
00:07:59,320 --> 00:08:01,300
Yeah, that's the perfect way to describe it.

100
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:13,560
The tuple also encompasses the pipeline state, the governance protocols, meaning, you know, who actually holds the authority to act on or modify the system at any given time and its recovery capacity.

101
00:08:14,020 --> 00:08:16,980
It fundamentally changed the definition of the object itself.

102
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:20,320
I mean, a car is no longer just the metal box sitting in your driveway.

103
00:08:20,320 --> 00:08:38,540
In this framework, the car is the vehicle, plus the factory that manufactured it, the entire global supply chain that sourced its parts, the maintenance logbook of every mechanic who ever tightened a bolt on it, and the emergency brake system all rolled into one conceptual entity.

104
00:08:38,780 --> 00:08:39,540
It's massive.

105
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:42,720
And the most vital component of this concept is order.

106
00:08:42,980 --> 00:08:43,260
Order.

107
00:08:43,480 --> 00:08:44,280
Okay, explain that.

108
00:08:44,280 --> 00:08:49,620
Well, a system history is not a chaotic bucket of random snapshots just thrown together in a folder.

109
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:50,040
Yeah.

110
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:53,540
It is an ordered, sequential trajectory through time.

111
00:08:53,720 --> 00:08:53,980
Right.

112
00:08:54,380 --> 00:09:02,700
The sequence in which data was acquired, or the precise chronological order in which interventions and updates occurred, is the defining characteristic of the system.

113
00:09:03,420 --> 00:09:07,380
Flyxion explicitly warns against treating history as an unordered set.

114
00:09:07,520 --> 00:09:08,840
Because the path matters.

115
00:09:09,100 --> 00:09:12,020
The path the system took dictates its fundamental reality.

116
00:09:12,020 --> 00:09:17,760
You know, order completely changes the outcome of a complex process, even if the ingredients are exactly the same.

117
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:18,340
Oh, for sure.

118
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:24,180
Like, if you are baking, right, and you mix the flour and water before adding the yeast, you get bread.

119
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:30,700
But if you heat the water to boiling, add the yeast, which kills it, and then add the flour, you get a dense, inedible brick.

120
00:09:31,140 --> 00:09:34,320
The ingredients, the state, are identical in the bowl.

121
00:09:34,400 --> 00:09:35,260
The state is identical.

122
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:40,780
But the sequence of operations resulted in a fundamentally different final entity.

123
00:09:40,780 --> 00:09:42,740
That's a really clear way to put it.

124
00:09:43,160 --> 00:09:52,720
Flyxion is taking the idea of admissibility, which usually just means a single data point satisfies a set of constraints and stretching it across the dimension of time.

125
00:09:53,080 --> 00:09:53,220
Right.

126
00:09:53,440 --> 00:09:59,020
So an admissible system is one whose entire chronological journey satisfies the safety constraints.

127
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:04,040
The paper defines this as A of H-sub-sigma equals one.

